<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philo of Alexandria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 20 BCE–c. 50 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> was a Jewish philosopher who lived in Alexandria and wrote in Greek. He interpreted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> scriptures allegorically in the light of Greek, especially Platonic, philosophy, and his works influenced early Christian theologians. Around 40 CE he led an embassy of Alexandrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to the emperor Gaius (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a>) after violent riots in the city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philo-of-alexandria.jpg" alt="Philo of Alexandria" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: André Thévet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PhiloThevet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philo's dates of birth and death are unknown. He called himself old when he joined the delegation to the emperor Gaius Caligula, and a reference to an event under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> suggests that he died between 45 and 50 CE. His family was noble and wealthy. Either his father or his paternal grandfather was granted Roman citizenship by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> wrote that he came from a priestly family. His brother Alexander Lysimachus was the tax administrator of customs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, a friend of the emperor Claudius and, it was said, the richest man in the city.</p><p>Philo was educated in the Hellenistic culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in the traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and in Greek philosophy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> was his largest philosophical influence, above all the Timaeus and the Phaedrus, and he also drew on the Stoics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a>. He read the Jewish scriptures chiefly in the Greek Septuagint, and how much <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> he knew is debated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He lived in an era of ethnic tension in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> condemned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> for a supposed alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, while Rome sought to suppress Jewish identity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, the Alexandrian Jewish community chose Philo as their chief representative before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> after civil disorder between Jews and Greeks. Josephus says that he and the community refused to treat the emperor as a god or to build statues, altars and temples for him. This is the only event of his life that can be dated with certainty. Philo also recounts that he visited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> at least once.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Philo wrote in Greek on philosophy, politics and religion. He used allegory to harmonise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, mainly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, with Greek philosophy, following the practice of both Jewish exegesis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stoicism">Stoicism</a>, though he also often favoured a literal reading. He held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> to be the source of all truth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> its true medium of revelation. His God is transcendent, without physical features or human emotions, unchangeable and beyond time and space. His Logos has a special relation to humans, as an interpreter of God's designs and a pattern for the shaping of the mind.</p><p>About fifty of his works survive and twenty to twenty-five are lost. Most are commentaries on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, among them the Quaestiones on Genesis and Exodus, which survive in Armenian, an Allegorical Commentary on Genesis and an Exposition of the Law. Others include On the Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> and the historical works Ad Flaccum and De legatione ad Gaium.</p><p>Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-fathers">Church Fathers</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-of-alexandria">Clement of Alexandria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a> used his work to connect the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> with philosophy, and Eusebius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a> cited him extensively. Rabbinic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> gave him little attention.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jewish thinkers, from <b>Philo of Alexandria</b> and Maimonides to Spinoza, Moses Mendelssohn and Martin Buber, contributed to philosophy, and Jews have been prominent far beyond their numbers in modern science, medicine, literature, music and scholarship.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=25" aria-label="Alexandria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5322 1027 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Philo of Alexandria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5772 1297h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5772 1297h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=25">What was here in 25 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
